netflix's cowboy bebop is like a  live-action jam session riffing on the  much-loved original anime about a bunch  of bounty hunters in space yupikai movie  lovers i'm jan and in this video i'm  explaining the jazziest differences  between the new show and the animated  classic at 49 years of age john cho may  be over 20 years older than his  character spike spiegel was in the  original anime but what the actor lacks  in youth he more than makes up for with  acres of effortless cool as the show's  lead space cowboy cho exudes laid-back  charisma and rye wit breathing  entertaining life into spike's often  cynical and cavalier approach to the  world while also managing to tap into  his darkness and vulnerability  interestingly portraying vulnerability  was something spike's english language  voice actor in the anime steve blum said  he found especially difficult and that's  the benefit john cho sees of coming to  the role as a more mature actor as he  feels he's better now at showing  weakness and vulnerability and love so  is more able to do justice to the  emotional depth they've tried to give  spike as for creating spike's look cho  trained hard to become what he calls  functionally rather than show horse  athletic to get a good performance and  also developed a character walk for the  first time in his career by watching how  spike moves in the original series and  that hair is cho's own grown out rather  than a wig which he was keen to avoid so  it would feel and look more natural and  wouldn't pull on his scalp during the  show's many action scenes  equally superb is mustafa shakir as  former cop jet black fans of marvel's  luke cage will doubtless remember the  actor as bushmaster in season two of  that show in cowboy bebop shakir brings  his anime counterpart to pitch perfect  life with a scene it all before  gruffness mixed with heart and humor as  well as great on-screen chemistry with  cho this time around a young daughter's  included in jet's backstory adding a  personal layer to the professional hurt  he suffered in his past while that's not  really necessary to get us on board with  his character it does lead to a small  but emotionally devastating moment for  him as well as some amusing moments  along the way my favorite of which is  him watching his daughter's school  performance in the foreground totally  oblivious to spike fighting a slew of  baddies alone in the background  the third member of the b-bop's crew fey  valentine arrives on the scene a bit  earlier than in the anime and given  daniela pineda's fantastic turn as fae  it's absolutely for the best pineda's  take on the bold brash amnesiac brings a  sense of wicked fun to the show whenever  she's on screen whether she's bantering  with spike and jet or getting herself or  others into and out of crazy jams like  in the ridiculously good seventh episode  with the odd exception faye doesn't keep  abandoning the team as their animated  counterpart often did which improves the  chemistry she has with her fellow bounty  hunters in fact all three lead actors  work brilliantly together producing a  truly entertaining dynamic plus pineda  imbues faye's search for her missing  past with a quiet melancholy and this  will be interesting to see how her hunt  for answers pans out in season two as  for her appearance obviously she was  going to look different in the real  world and her updated outfit reflects  that while nodding to the original  if you've watched amazon's superhero  series the boys you'll likely recognize  actor alex hassell who played  translucent in that show and stars as  vicious in cowboy bebop how much you  enjoy his turn here depends on how  theatrical you like your villains  compared to the anime we see much more  of vicious in the live-action series  given he's the show's main antagonist  building up his character and expanding  his backstory makes sense as it makes  him feel much more obviously integral to  the story from the get-go but the  penultimate episode that lays out his  journey to becoming a syndicate capo and  his relationship with spike is one of  the less interesting ones as it feels  like we've either been told or intuited  enough already so it ends up seeming  like an over long interruption before  the big finale by the way if you're  wondering what happened to vicious pet  cormorant from the anime the new show's  creative team made an early decision to  give it the chop which is understandable  given the added vfx cost to create  something that's not exactly crucial  however they did include various easter  eggs to the bird on vicious costume  julia who is vicious wife and spike's  former flame also gets a lot more screen  time than she did in the original series  where she was more enigmatic but she  still mainly exists just to be part of a  love triangle and actress elena sateen  does what she can with a character who's  mostly there to appear fragile and  sultry fortunately there's a hint of  more interesting times ahead given where  the show leaves her character and i'm  intrigued to see where the creators take  things in season two by the way satine  gets to showcase her singing and  according to john cho also plays the  piano herself in the series performing a  new song that's important for julia  similar to the anime welsh corgi and  datadog ein turns up in the lives of the  bebop crew thanks to a run-in with a pet  napper named hakeem in the live-action  series two super cute canines harry and  charlie bring iron to absolutely  adorable life though according to the  show's two male leads they were pretty  heavy to carry around as well as the  real life pups john cho says he also  spent a lot of time acting opposite a  stuffed toy dog to get his eye alignment  right in scenes where ein was off screen  in terms of how the show's adapted  overall it gets off to a delightfully  playful wacky start with spike and jess  at a casino on the hunt for their latest  criminal cash cow the scene is a joy to  watch from spike's elevator entrance and  fancy fighting footwork including a  casino chip dropkick to jet's arrival  crash landing through the ceiling to the  duo's bickering best mates back and  forth rather than simply remaking the  original episodes or creating an  entirely different narrative with the  same core characters the live-action  series aims to retain the anime spirit  and takes a pick and mix approach to its  plots and characters in other words they  get everybody in the stuff together but  in a variety of ways for instance the  first episode takes a similar attack to  the anime pilot introducing asimov and  katarina and their attempted red-eye  drug deal but in a change it also  introduces fae how the creative team  re-jigs or remixes elements often works  but at times they don't quite pull it  off for example i was glad i already  knew the anime when i watched the  eco-terrorism episode with maria murdock  because the live-action take on that  felt a bit too rushed to fully take him  properly each episode gives the bebop  crew fresh criminals to bounty hunt like  in the anime however this version takes  more regular time from the start to  expand on the crew's histories so those  episodes feel less like standalone  stories and more clearly like part of a  bigger overarching narrative the series  is packed with easter eggs and hat tips  to the original from building names to  complete shots and composer yoko kano's  back with new music as well as old  favorites including her iconic opening  theme tune thank goodness because i  really love that  spoilers now for the season one ending  the appearance of hacking wiz kid  radical edward was teased a few times  this season i got a lead from radical  egg but ed only appears right at the  very end even though it's just a brief  glimpse we can see this version has the  signature look and the hyper child-like  energy and smiley eccentricity you'd  expect however fan reactions have been  mi